# How fast did solar panels get cheaper?
subtitle: "Draw where the price of solar panels went after 2008, then see the crash: from $132 a watt in 1975 to about 27 cents today."

## How fast did solar panels get cheaper?
use: line_chart
with:
    x:
      - 2000
      - 2004
      - 2006
      - 2008
      - 2010
      - 2012
      - 2014
      - 2016
      - 2018
      - 2020
      - 2022
      - 2024
    series:
      - {values: [6.5, 4.7, 5.17, 4.75, 2.51, 1.11, 0.79, 0.68, 0.51, 0.37, 0.37, 0.27], name: Solar panels}
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 8
    y_label: $ per watt
    points: true
sources:
    - id: owid
      title: Solar (photovoltaic) panel prices
      authors: Our World in Data, from Nemet (2009), Farmer & Lafond (2016) and IRENA
      year: 2025
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/solar-pv-prices
      finding: 'module price per watt in 2025 dollars: $132 in 1975, $6.50 in 2000, $4.75 in 2008, $0.27 in 2024'
    - id: iea
      title: World Energy Outlook 2020
      authors: International Energy Agency
      year: 2020
      finding: solar PV now offers some of the lowest-cost electricity ever seen

    dot endpt #b5482e at.data [2024,0.27] hidden true
    text end "$0.27" above endpt gap 40 #b5482e cites ["owid"] font_size 48 hidden true
    text drop "−94% since 2008" #b5482e cites ["owid"] x 640 y 470 font_size 52 hidden true
    text fact "giant factories" #2f6b8f x 560 y 315 font_size 40 hidden true
    text swanson "×2 panels → −20% price" #2f6b8f x 640 y 400 font_size 40 hidden true
    text y1975 "1975: $132 ↑" #b5482e cites ["owid"] x 640 y 625 font_size 52 hidden true
    text total "−99.8% since 1975" #b5482e cites ["owid"] x 640 y 540 font_size 52 hidden true

    card title "How fast did solar get cheaper?"

How fast have solar panels become cheaper? Here is the price of one watt of panel, in today's dollars.
    draw axes

    ask "What happened next? Draw the line on to 2024." on line_1 from 2008 store g tolerance 0.08
        default: 4.4,4,3.6,3.2,2.9,2.6,2.4,2.2
        intro: In 2000 a watt cost about six and a half dollars. By 2008 it was still almost five.
        right: Close: you were {g.off} dollars off on average. The price crashed, to about a quarter of a dollar.
        wrong: You were {g.off} dollars off on average. The price crashed: by 2024 a watt cost about twenty-seven cents.

That is a fall of about ninety-four percent in sixteen years, to twenty-seven cents.
    draw endpt end drop

After 2008, giant factories, many of them in China, pushed the price down faster than ever.
    draw fact

The rule of thumb, called Swanson's law: every doubling of all the panels ever made cuts the price by about a fifth.
    draw swanson

That is why the International Energy Agency calls solar some of the cheapest electricity ever seen.
    highlight end

And the story starts earli (@draw y1975@)er. In 1975, the same watt cost about a hundred and thirty-two dollars, far off the top of this chart.

    erase drop fact swanson

From then to now, the  (@draw total@)price has fallen by more than ninety-nine percent.
---
